                <h2>A Dashboard Is an Operations Workspace, Not Just a Data Screen</h2>
<p>Agency account-opening services manage multiple countries, advertising platforms, and client types daily. When progress is tracked only in spreadsheets, chat groups, and individual memory, teams risk duplicate submissions, unanswered reviews, incomplete access handovers, and unclear billing responsibilities. Yiyu recommends a dashboard that connects five information sets: clients, orders, accounts, funds, and after-sales tasks.</p>
<h3>1. Client Intake: Confirm the Scope of Service</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Are the client entity, target regions, platforms, and number of accounts clearly defined?</li>
<li>[ ] Does the industry involve financial services, healthcare, gambling, crypto, or other higher-risk categories?</li>
<li>[ ] Can the client provide valid identity, entity, and business documentation?</li>
<li>[ ] Does the order specify whether the service covers account opening only or also funding, access configuration, and campaign management?</li>
<li>[ ] Have fees, potential policy changes, and refund conditions been confirmed?</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Example: A tool-based app initially defined “successful opening” as receiving a confirmation email. Because the client and agency interpreted activation, first funding, and access setup differently, the team introduced four delivery checkpoints. This reduced avoidable disputes.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>2. Review Follow-Up: Assign an Owner and Deadline</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Do submissions meet naming, formatting, and document-quality requirements?</li>
<li>[ ] Are submission time, review status, rejection reasons, and next actions recorded?</li>
<li>[ ] Are overdue tasks automatically flagged and escalated?</li>
<li>[ ] Does the system prevent duplicate orders or repeated submissions for the same client?</li>
<li>[ ] Are document versions retained so outdated files are not reused?</li>
</ul>
<p>The dashboard should distinguish “information pending,” “submitted,” “under review,” “changes required,” “approved,” and “rejected,” rather than relying only on “in progress” and “completed.”</p>
<h3>3. Account Delivery: Complete a Pre-Launch Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Has normal activation been confirmed, with the correct entity and currency?</li>
<li>[ ] Has the client received only the required access, while temporary operators have been removed?</li>
<li>[ ] Have campaign access, pixels, billing methods, and budget controls been verified?</li>
<li>[ ] Are balance alerts, funding records, and invoice downloads in place?</li>
<li>[ ] Is there a documented check for suspicious logins, chargeback risks, or prohibited content?</li>
</ul>
<h2>Connect Funding, Balance, and After-Sales to the Same Order</h2>
<p>Account opening is not the end of the service. Funding requests, receipts, actual spend, and balance alerts should be linked to the relevant order.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Before funding:</strong> Verify the account, currency, amount, paying entity, and payment method.</li>
<li><strong>During funding:</strong> Track pending, credited, failed, or reconciliation-required status, and retain a transaction reference.</li>
<li><strong>After funding:</strong> Confirm that the balance has arrived and that accounting records match platform statements.</li>
<li><strong>Alerts:</strong> Distinguish insufficient balance, abnormal spend, and failed charges instead of using one generic warning.</li>
<li><strong>Reconciliation:</strong> Compare records by client, platform, currency, and billing period rather than only matching the total funded amount.</li>
</ul>
<p>A fixed alert threshold is rarely enough. E-commerce teams with fast seasonal consumption can set multiple levels based on average daily spend and payment cycles. Multi-account clients should be checked individually, because a healthy total balance may conceal an account about to lose delivery.</p>
<h2>Team Permissions and Audit Controls</h2>
<p>Operations staff should not automatically see every client’s asset information. Sales, review, account operations, finance, and administrator roles should be separated. The following controls should be checked:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Are viewing, editing, exporting, funding, and refund actions assigned different permissions?</li>
<li>[ ] Are client records encrypted, and are exported files protected by expiration rules?</li>
<li>[ ] Are passwords, verification codes, and recovery email addresses prohibited from ordinary notes?</li>
<li>[ ] Do critical status changes record the operator, timestamp, and previous and updated values?</li>
<li>[ ] Are access rights removed promptly when staff leave or change roles?</li>
<li>[ ] Are overdue tasks and incomplete client records reviewed regularly?</li>
</ul>
<h2>Weekly Review: Answer Five Questions</h2>
<ol>
<li>How many new clients were delayed by documentation issues this week?</li>
<li>Which documents or review stages caused most rejections?</li>
<li>Which accounts were approved but not yet delivered?</li>
<li>Were funding and unusual-spend alerts handled promptly?</li>
<li>Which topic caused the most repeated client questions: opening progress, balance, or access?</li>
</ol>
<p>A useful agency account-opening dashboard is traceable, transferable, and auditable. It gives clients visibility while giving operations staff a clear next action. Teams do not need to build every feature at once. Start by standardizing status labels, required fields, and exception rules, then gradually connect account, funding, and finance data.</p>
